  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 355 AM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nA well-developed line of storms is progressing through Minnesota \nearly this morning well ahead of a boundary to the west. These \nstorms have approached severe level winds, but have stayed \nwithin the 45 to 55 mph range for the strongest gusts. We expect\nstorms to continue into western WI, and eventually wane off the\nfurther they decouple from the LLJ. The hi-res CAMs have had a \nhorrible handle on the convection this morning, but the general \nidea of what we had been seeing with development early this \nmorning ended up being our solution with this first wave. As the\nmorning activity clears out to the southeast, it will be early \nenough for us to be able to recover ahead of our next chance for\nstorms later today.\n\nThere is some indication that we could have development near \nthe Minnesota River Valley in SW MN mid-late morning that could \nreach as far north as the metro, but it would be favored to stay\ncloser towards the Mankato and Owatonna area. All of this \npotential storm activity will have an impact on temperatures and\nheat indices today. With the surge of more humid air to the \nsouth of the front, heat indices have the potential to climb \ninto the triple digits for areas in southern MN during the early\nto mid afternoon. Even as the front moves through, the drier \nair could allow for more efficient mixing of warmer temperatures\ndown to the surface. The urban heat island of the Twin Cities \nmetro may see a period of heat impacts as well, but the boundary\nmoving through in the afternoon should allow for quicker \nrecovery this evening. There was little change to the SPC severe\nweather outlook for today, but main risk continues to favor \nareas south and east. After our morning storms, it seems much \nmore likely that the majority of the Upper MS Valley remains \ndry. That said, the best chance for any storm development this \nafternoon would be between Rusk to Eau Claire Counties and east \ninto central WI. Tied to the front will be a wave of some \nadditional wildfire smoke, but it does not look to be as dense \nor long-lasting.\n\nBehind this wave, cooler and less humid air will settle in.\nAfter over a week of consecutive 90+ degree days, highs will \nreturn closer to normal for mid week. We should also see \nnighttime lows drop into the 50s nearly areawide Tuesday and \nWednesday night, allowing for a nice time to open up windows \nagain. Deterministic and ensemble guidance favor a much drier \npattern under northwest flow and a building by the end of the \nweek. Any rain chances will come from more mesoscale features, \nand will be day to day. The main thing to watch with the \nbuilding ridge this weekend, is our highs may climb right back \nup into the 90s again.",
